<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haya people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haya are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of the Kagera region in north-western Tanzania, on the western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>. For several centuries before colonial rule they were organised in small kingdoms such as Kiziba, Kyamutwara and Ihangiro, ruled by royal clans with sacred drums. They grow bananas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, and archaeology shows that their ancestors smelted iron in sophisticated furnaces some 2,000 years ago.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haya-people.jpg" alt="Haya people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Happiness Stephen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:African-haya_girl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
